WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.942
In everyday language, work can sound like trying hard.

2
00:00:04.942 --> 00:00:09.883
In physics, work has a sharper meaning.

3
00:00:09.883 --> 00:00:14.825
Work is done when a force causes displacement in the direction of that force.

4
00:00:14.825 --> 00:00:19.766
That is why the answer is measured in joules.

5
00:00:19.766 --> 00:00:24.708
A joule here is not just a calculation label.

6
00:00:24.708 --> 00:00:29.649
It is a statement about energy transferred.

7
00:00:29.649 --> 00:00:34.591
Once that meaning is secure, the lesson becomes much calmer.

8
00:00:34.591 --> 00:00:39.532
The learner stops thinking about strain or effort and starts asking the real question: did the object move in the force direction, and if so, how far?

9
00:00:39.532 --> 00:00:44.474
Keep this takeaway in view: In this lesson, work means transferred energy, not just trying hard.

10
00:00:44.474 --> 00:00:49.415
Work means energy transfer.

11
00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:54.357
Work tells how much energy a force transfers to an object.

12
00:00:54.357 --> 00:00:59.298
Need means force and movement.

13
00:00:59.298 --> 00:01:04.240
A force must actually cause displacement for work to be done.

14
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:10.176
This picture is important because it separates force from work.

15
00:01:10.176 --> 00:01:16.111
A crate that moves in the direction of the pull can gain energy through work.

16
00:01:16.111 --> 00:01:22.047
A wall that does not move may still be pushed hard, but the work done on the wall by that force is zero because there is no displacement.

17
00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:27.982
That is one of the most useful corrections in the whole lesson.

18
00:01:27.982 --> 00:01:33.918
Force alone is not enough.

19
00:01:33.918 --> 00:01:39.853
Motion in the relevant direction has to happen, otherwise the work statement never gets started.

20
00:01:39.853 --> 00:01:45.789
Keep this takeaway in view: Work depends on displacement, not on force presence alone.

21
00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:51.724
Moving crate: Force and displacement line up, so energy transfer through work can happen.

22
00:01:51.724 --> 00:01:57.660
Stuck wall: The force is real, but no displacement means zero work on the wall.

23
00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.806
Imagine pushing a trolley through a supermarket aisle.

24
00:02:02.806 --> 00:02:07.953
If the trolley rolls forward, your push transfers energy and changes the motion story.

25
00:02:07.953 --> 00:02:13.099
If the wheels are locked and it does not move, you may feel effort, but the trolley has not received work through displacement in the push direction.

26
00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:18.245
That is exactly the physics distinction.

27
00:02:18.245 --> 00:02:23.392
The analogy helps because it keeps the lesson tied to a visible cause-and-effect picture instead of an isolated equation.

28
00:02:23.392 --> 00:02:28.538
Keep this takeaway in view: The question is not only how hard the push is.

29
00:02:28.538 --> 00:02:33.685
The question is what displacement it causes.

30
00:02:33.685 --> 00:02:38.831
Push and roll means work happens.

31
00:02:38.831 --> 00:02:43.977
The trolley moves in the direction of the push.

32
00:02:43.977 --> 00:02:49.124
Push and no roll means zero work on trolley.

33
00:02:49.124 --> 00:02:54.270
Without displacement, there is no work done on that object by the push.

34
00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:59.975
The core relation is work equals force times distance moved in the direction of the force.

35
00:02:59.975 --> 00:03:05.680
So when the force and displacement line up, multiply those two quantities and read the answer as transferred energy.

36
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:11.385
The formula is useful because it compresses the story into one line, but the story still comes first.

37
00:03:11.385 --> 00:03:17.090
If the movement condition is missing, the learner should not multiply automatically.

38
00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:22.795
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula works best when the physical meaning is already visible.

39
00:03:22.795 --> 00:03:28.500
F: The force causing the motion.

40
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:34.205
s: The distance moved in the force direction.

41
00:03:34.205 --> 00:03:39.910
W = F x s.

42
00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:45.615
joules = newtons x metres.

43
00:03:45.615 --> 00:03:51.320
Use the distance moved in the direction of the force.

44
00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:56.438
If the same force moves an object twice as far in the same direction, twice as much work is done.

45
00:03:56.438 --> 00:04:01.556
If the distance is halved, the work is halved.

46
00:04:01.556 --> 00:04:06.675
This is a direct scaling relationship.

47
00:04:06.675 --> 00:04:11.793
That is useful because many work questions can be understood before they are calculated.

48
00:04:11.793 --> 00:04:16.911
The learner should already expect a bigger distance or bigger force to give a bigger transferred energy when the direction condition stays the same.

49
00:04:16.911 --> 00:04:22.029
Keep this takeaway in view: Use scaling logic before arithmetic.

50
00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:27.147
Bigger matching force or distance means bigger work.

51
00:04:27.147 --> 00:04:32.265
Same force, more distance means more work.

52
00:04:32.265 --> 00:04:37.384
More displacement in the force direction means more energy transferred.

53
00:04:37.384 --> 00:04:42.502
Same distance, more force means more work.

54
00:04:42.502 --> 00:04:47.620
A stronger force transfers more energy over the same path.

55
00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:51.307
Try three checks.

56
00:04:51.307 --> 00:04:54.994
If a force moves an object farther in the same direction, what happens to the work done?

57
00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:58.681
It increases.

58
00:04:58.681 --> 00:05:02.368
If the object does not move, what is the work done by that force on that object?

59
00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:06.055
Zero.

60
00:05:06.055 --> 00:05:09.742
And why is the answer measured in joules?

61
00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:13.428
Because the result is an amount of energy transferred.

62
00:05:13.428 --> 00:05:17.115
Those are the three ideas that hold the whole lesson together.

63
00:05:17.115 --> 00:05:20.802
Keep this takeaway in view: Work, movement, and energy transfer now fit together as one idea.

64
00:05:20.802 --> 00:05:24.489
More matching distance means more work.

65
00:05:24.489 --> 00:05:28.176
The energy transfer grows with the displacement.

66
00:05:28.176 --> 00:05:31.863
No movement means zero work.

67
00:05:31.863 --> 00:05:35.550
Force without displacement does not produce work on that object.

68
00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:40.103
You are ready for the worked example now.

69
00:05:40.103 --> 00:05:44.656
A 20 newton force pulls a crate 6 metres along the floor in the same direction as the pull.

70
00:05:44.656 --> 00:05:49.209
The movement condition is satisfied, so use work equals force times distance.

71
00:05:49.209 --> 00:05:53.761
Twenty times six gives 120 joules.

72
00:05:53.761 --> 00:05:58.314
Do not stop at the number.

73
00:05:58.314 --> 00:06:02.867
State what it means.

74
00:06:02.867 --> 00:06:07.420
The pulling force has transferred 120 joules of energy to the crate.

75
00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:11.973
That final sentence is what turns the calculation into physics.

76
00:06:11.973 --> 00:06:16.526
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: calculate the work, then say what energy transfer it represents.

77
00:06:16.526 --> 00:06:21.079
Step 1: Check that the crate moves in the pull direction.

78
00:06:21.079 --> 00:06:25.631
Step 2: Multiply force by distance to get the work done.

79
00:06:25.631 --> 00:06:30.184
W = F x s.

80
00:06:30.184 --> 00:06:34.737
W = 20 x 6 = 120 J.

81
00:06:34.737 --> 00:06:39.290
Read the final answer as transferred energy.
